 "postHocFindings (post hoc, not part of the rule)": [
  "post hoc: the ordering constraint does not remove round 7's second peak, it only re-labels it. The peak where the answers are explained as the upper tail of a broad low component reappears as mu1 just above mu0 (mu1 about 0.03 to 0.5 in z, pi 0.3 to 0.5, s1 ~0.5-1.5). It often has a HIGHER log-likelihood than the separated solution (e.g. window courts fresh-2|5: -2048.97 vs -2067.41; poolA courts fresh-2|5: -2424.21 vs -2447.06; regulation everywhere), so the highest-likelihood rule picks it on purpose. That mode accepts hundreds of articles: window courts fresh-2|5 accepted 708 (T11F 0.009 vs truncated 0.769), regulation fresh-2|5 803.",
  "post hoc: so round 7's failure was not an optimiser artefact but a property of the truncated likelihood with few answers: the selection term rewards a model in which nearly everything above the cut-off is 'relevant-ish'. One start (round 7) usually stayed in the separated basin by luck of the start; more starts find the worse-for-filtering but better-for-likelihood mode. Candidate remedies (not tested): a prior or bound on pi / minimum separation, or the truncated term only for the not-relevant answers.",
  "post hoc: start 2 (delta = log 1, mu1 = 1) is the one that reaches the overlapping mode; starts 1 and 3 mostly agree with round 7's fit. Distinct optima across the three starts (loglik spread > 1e-4): {\"window\": {\"unitsFitted\": 56, \"unitsWithDistinctOptima\": 27}, \"poolA\": {\"unitsFitted\": 32, \"unitsWithDistinctOptima\": 20}, \"poolB\": {\"unitsFitted\": 32, \"unitsWithDistinctOptima\": 18}}. The winner counts in orderedWinningStart include exact ties within 1e-6, where the winner is arbitrary.",
